  ],
  "text": "Driving a car is not hard. Handling the thing nobody anticipated is the entire problem. A car has three ways of seeing, and none of them fully agree. Cameras see detail and colour, and are ruined by low sun and heavy rain. Radar sees straight through weather and cannot tell you what the thing actually is. Lidar builds an exact shape of everything nearby, and struggles in spray and fog. So the system merges all three and decides which to believe. That part mostly works. Ninety nine percent of driving is easy. Ninety nine percent is also useless. It is the last fraction that matters. Someone waving you through. A lane painted over twice. A plastic bag that behaves like a rock, and a rock that behaves like a bag. There is no list of those. Which is exactly why the timelines keep slipping. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
